vely involved in research and the development of guidelines within these disciplines. Additionally, Alcaide J is recognized as one of the authors referenced in this important manual, highlighting their contribution to the literature on endocrinology and nutrition.", "FERNÁNDEZ GARCÍA JC:Fernández García JC is a notable contributor to the field of endocrinology and nutrition, recognized as one of the authors of the \"Manual de Endocrinología y Nutrición.\" This manual, published by the Sociedad Española de Endocrinología y Nutrición, showcases the expertise of Fernández García JC alongside other contributors. His work is referenced within the manual, highlighting his significant role in advancing knowledge in this specialized area of medicine.", "HIPERTRIGLICERIDEMIA FAMILIAR:Hipertrigliceridemia familiar is a genetic disorder characterized by elevated triglyceride levels in the blood. This condition is transmitted as an autosomal dominant trait, meaning that it can be inherited from just one affected parent.", "HIPERLIPIDEMIA FAMILIAR COMBINADA:\"Hiperlipidemia familiar combinada\" es un trastorno genético que afecta entre el 0,5% y el 1% de la población norteamericana. Se caracteriza por una captación reducida de ácidos grasos libres y una sobreproducción de apolipoproteína B y triglicéridos, manifestándose en forma de lipoproteínas de muy baja densidad. Esta condición se hereda de manera autosómica dominante y se asocia con niveles elevados de lípidos en la sangre, incluyendo hipertrigliceridemia y colesterol elevado. Además, puede presentarse como una hiperlipidemia mixta, que puede incluir hipertrigliceridemia aislada o niveles elevados de colesterol. La hiperlipidemia familiar combinada está relacionada con un alto riesgo cardiovascular, lo que la convierte en una preocupación significativa para la salud de quienes la padecen.", "XANTOMAS TENDINOSOS:Xantomas tendinosos are cholesterol deposits that can occur in tendons, often associated with certain types of hyperlipidemia.", "ARCO CORNEAL:Arco corneal refers to a ring around the cornea that can indicate high cholesterol levels, often seen in hyperlipidemia conditions.", "APOLIPOPROTEÍNA E:La apolipoproteína E es una proteína crucial en el catabolismo de los quilomicrones y restos de VLDL, con diferentes genotipos que afectan la dislipidemia.", "DISBETALIPOPROTEINEMIA:Disbetalipoproteinemia is a rare lipid disorder primarily caused by homozygosity in the apolipoprotein E (apo E) gene. This condition is characterized by a mutation in the apo E gene, which significantly affects the metabolism of lipoproteins. As a result, individuals with disbetalipoproteinemia exhibit elevated levels of triglycerides and very low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) cholesterol, as well as the presence of chylomicrons in fasting states.", "XANTOMAS:Xantomas are fatty deposits that can form on the skin and are indicative of lipid disorders, such as hypertriglyceridemia and dyslipidemia. These deposits are often associated with metabolic disorders and can occur in individuals with high cholesterol levels, particularly in cases of familial hypercholesterolemia and dysbetalipoproteinemia. Overall, xantomas serve as a manifestation of underlying lipid abnormalities in affected patients.", "LIPOPROTEÍNAS DE MUY BAJA DENSIDAD:Las lipoproteínas de muy baja densidad (VLDL) son un tipo de lipoproteína que se produce en el hígado y está asociada con el transporte de triglicéridos en la sangre.", "APOLIPOPROTEÍNA B:La apolipoproteína B es una proteína que se asocia con lipoproteínas y es un marcador importante en el diagnóstico de dislipidemias.", "LIPID DISORDERS:Lipid disorders refer to abnormalities in lipid metabolism, which can result in conditions such as hyperlipidemia and are often managed through dietary changes and medications.", "CHOLESTEROL:Cholesterol is a lipid that serves as an important indicator of nutritional status and overall health. It is a waxy substance found in the blood, essential for building cells. However, while cholesterol is necessary for various bodily functions, high levels can lead to significant health issues, including heart disease.", "HYPERLIPIDEMIA:Hyperlipidemia is a medical condition characterized by elevated levels of lipids in the blood. This condition poses significant health risks, particularly increasing the likelihood of cardiovascular diseases. It is especially concerning for patients receiving intravenous lipids and those who have undergone liver transplantation. Management of hyperlipidemia can often be achieved through dietary changes, as supported by various studies."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "temperature": 0.0, "frequency_penalty": null, "presence_penalty": null, "top_p": 1.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1}, "history": null}